1) The yield on 30-year US Treasuries rose to 5.32% - its highest level since 2007;
2) Germany's 10-year Bund yield reached 3.25% - its highest level since 2011;
3) France's 30-year bond yield reached 4.90% - its highest level since 2008;
4) Japan's 10-year JGB yield reached 2.95% - its highest level since 1996.

Markets are simultaneously pricing in higher inflation risk, widening deficits, increased government debt issuance, high oil prices, and a higher long-term risk premium. In Japan, expectations of further BOJ rate hikes are also rising.

This is a negative macro signal for risk assets. Rising long-term yields, especially real yields, increase the cost of capital and make risk-free bonds more competitive with equities and cryptocurrencies. Technology companies, high-multiple assets, and Bitcoin face the greatest pressure. If yields continue to rise, global financial conditions will tighten further even without additional rate hikes from central banks.
